RIGHTS OF VICTIMS
Article 4
(1) Every victim of gender-based violence and children shall have the right to:
a. obtain protection for personal, family, and property security, and be free from threats related to testimony that will be, is being, or has been given;
b. participate in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;
c. obtain information regarding case developments and court decisions;
d. receive integrated services that are fast, appropriate, comfortable, and in accordance with needs;
e. recovery and social reintegration; and
f. receive legal assistance, psychological support, spiritual guidance, economic support, social support and interpreter services.
(2) The rights of victims as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER IV
OBLIGATIONS AND AUTHORITIES OF REGIONAL GOVERNMENT
Article 5
(1) In providing protection to victims of gender-based violence and children, the Regional Government has the obligation to:
a. prevent the occurrence of violence;
b. provide protection for victims of violence;
c. provide recovery services and social reintegration for victims;
d. encourage and increase community participation;
e. cooperate with service providers in efforts to prevent, protect and recover victims of violence;
f. supervise the implementation of services for victims involving the community; and
g. provide safe houses (shelters).
(2) To implement the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government has the authority to:
a. formulate policies and programs on the elimination of gender-based violence and violence against children;
b. facilitate the implementation of integrated services;
c. provide facilities and infrastructure;
d. increase the capacity of service provider institutions;
e. conduct coordination and cooperation in the implementation of protection for victims of gender-based violence and violence against children;
f. encourage community participation; and g. conduct monitoring and evaluation.
(3) The Regional Government in implementing the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) must pay attention to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, husbands, wives, or other persons who are legally responsible for the victim.

Article 13
(1) Increasing Community Participation as referred to in Article 7 paragraph (3) letter e is to provide the widest possible opportunity for the community to participate in assisting the prevention and handling of gender-based and child violence.
(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by:
a. providing protection for victims; b. providing emergency assistance;
c. providing advocacy to victims and or the community regarding the handling of gender-based and child violence cases;
d. assisting in the process of submitting applications for protection orders;
e. providing assistance in the form of money, goods, or services; and/or
f. conveying information to the authorized authorities related to gender-based and child violence cases.

CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
The protection of victims of gender-based violence and children is based on Pancasila and grounded in the 1945 Constitution, as well as basic principles that include:
a. non-discrimination;
b. best interests of victims;
c. gender justice and equality;
d. victim protection;
e. survival, and growth and development of children;
f. respect for the opinion of children; g. transparency;
h. integration;
i. empowerment; and
j. confidentiality of victims.

